// The standard library pages
// ==========================
// The `hive.*` half of what `hive agents` writes into `.hivedocs/`.
//
// A module is split when its parts are reached for separately: somebody writing a
// WebSocket server has no use for the TCP calls, so `net` and `crypto` are several
// pages each. `conv` and `time` are small and whole.
import ./page

// Every page about the standard library, bar `hive.ui`.
func pages(): page.Page[dyn] {
    return [
        page.Page("stdlib/README.md", index()),
        page.Page("stdlib/conv.md", conv()),
        page.Page("stdlib/math.md", math()),
        page.Page("stdlib/map.md", maps()),
        page.Page("stdlib/file.md", files()),
        page.Page("stdlib/image.md", image()),
        page.Page("stdlib/term.md", term()),
        page.Page("stdlib/term-exec.md", termExec()),
        page.Page("stdlib/env.md", env()),
        page.Page("stdlib/task.md", task()),
        page.Page("stdlib/time.md", times()),
        page.Page("stdlib/json.md", json()),
        page.Page("stdlib/crypto.md", crypto()),
        page.Page("stdlib/crypto-encrypt.md", cryptoEncrypt()),
        page.Page("stdlib/crypto-jwt.md", cryptoJwt()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net.md", net()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net-http.md", netHttp()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net-websockets.md", netWebsockets()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net-sockets.md", netSockets()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net-names.md", netNames()),
        page.Page("stdlib/net-urls.md", netUrls()),
        page.Page("stdlib/sql.md", sql()),
        page.Page("stdlib/syslink.md", syslink()),
        page.Page("stdlib/syslink-calls.md", syslinkCalls()),
        page.Page("stdlib/syslink-nodes.md", syslinkNodes()),
        page.Page("stdlib/audio.md", audio())
    ]
}

// --- The index ---------------------------------------------------------------

func index(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # The standard library

        ~BT~hive.<module>.<name>~BT~ works anywhere with no import; ~BT~<module>.<name>~BT~
        (~BT~net.httpRequest~BT~) works where the program has not taken the name
        ([modules](../modules.md)). ~BT~import hive.net as web~BT~ only adds a **spelling**:
        ~BT~web.httpRequest~BT~ is ~BT~hive.net.httpRequest~BT~, linked the same.

        Types live in their module (~BT~hive.net.HttpRequest~BT~, ~BT~hive.json.JsonError~BT~,
        ~BT~hive.map.Map~BT~, ~BT~hive.syslink.SyslinkError~BT~); only ~BT~Result~BT~, ~BT~Table~BT~ and
        ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ are on ~BT~hive~BT~.

        **Calls are held to their signature** (argument count and types). A type or
        enumeration variant the module lacks (~BT~hive.net.Nonesuch~BT~,
        ~BT~hive.ui.TextSize.Small()~BT~) is a compile error.

        **A failure is a variant.** A module that fails more than one way has a union
        error, one variant per way, each with a ~BT~message~BT~ and whatever else the module
        knows:

        ~CODE~hive
        if file.read(path) is Result.Error(file.FileError.NotFound(_, _)) \{
        	echo "nothing there yet"
        }
        ~CODE~

        These are ~BT~FileError~BT~, ~BT~HttpStreamError~BT~, ~BT~WsError~BT~, ~BT~SocketError~BT~, ~BT~NetError~BT~,
        ~BT~SqlError~BT~, ~BT~CryptoError~BT~, ~BT~SyslinkError~BT~, ~BT~TermError~BT~, ~BT~ProcessError~BT~ and the
        core's ~BT~SecretError~BT~. Each comes with its module, named or not, is matched and
        covered like any union, and prints as what went wrong and why.

        Codec errors (~BT~JsonError~BT~, ~BT~JwtError~BT~, ~BT~QueryParamsError~BT~, ~BT~FlagError~BT~) share
        ~BT~path~BT~, ~BT~expected~BT~ and ~BT~found~BT~: ~BT~Mismatch~BT~ for the wrong shape, ~BT~Broken~BT~ for
        breaking one of the type's [laws](../laws.md#a-types-laws).

        **Unused modules are not in the build.** All but two need only the target's
        standard library and build offline; ~BT~hive.sql~BT~ (database drivers) and
        ~BT~hive.ui.scene~BT~ (a pinned engine) are fetched on first need, cached, and linked
        only into programs that use them.

        | page | module | what is on it |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | [conv](conv.md) | ~BT~hive.conv~BT~ | rounding, widening, rendering, parsing |
        | [math](math.md) | ~BT~hive.math~BT~ | thirteen ~BT~Float~BT~ functions |
        | [map](map.md) | ~BT~hive.map~BT~ | the dictionary |
        | [file](file.md) | ~BT~hive.file~BT~ | the filesystem, where the program is, and what it carries in ~BT~assets/~BT~ |
        | [image](image.md) | ~BT~hive.image~BT~ | pictures read into their pixels, and resized |
        | [term](term.md) | ~BT~hive.term~BT~ | stdout, stderr, lines and keys of input, the window's size, arguments, exit |
        | [term-exec](term-exec.md) | ~BT~hive.term~BT~ | running another program, and one that runs alongside |
        | [env](env.md) | ~BT~hive.env~BT~ | the environment and ~BT~.env~BT~ |
        | [task](task.md) | ~BT~hive.task~BT~ | ~BT~sleep~BT~, ~BT~sleepForever~BT~, and where ~BT~with timeout~BT~ lives |
        | [time](time.md) | ~BT~hive.time~BT~ | Unix milliseconds and ~BT~strftime~BT~ |
        | [json](json.md) | ~BT~hive.json~BT~ | your types *are* the schema |
        | [crypto](crypto.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | hashing, encoding, random |
        | [crypto-encrypt](crypto-encrypt.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | ~BT~encrypt~BT~ / ~BT~decrypt~BT~ |
        | [crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | tokens, as a codec |
        | [net](net.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | what every server here has in common |
        | [net-http](net-http.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | HTTP, client and server |
        | [net-websockets](net-websockets.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | WebSockets |
        | [net-sockets](net-sockets.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | raw TCP |
        | [net-names](net-names.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | ~BT~resolve~BT~ and ~BT~localAddress~BT~ |
        | [net-urls](net-urls.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | ~BT~urlEncode~BT~ and the query string codec |
        | [sql](sql.md) | ~BT~hive.sql~BT~ | connections, drivers, and running a query |
        | [syslink](syslink.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | what a service is |
        | [syslink-calls](syslink-calls.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | every call, and calling an address |
        | [syslink-nodes](syslink-nodes.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | across machines |
        | [ui](ui.md) | ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ | a view is a value — **thirteen pages**, start here |
        | [audio](audio.md) | ~BT~hive.audio~BT~ | a recording or a live stream, by name |

        Tables from a CSV or spreadsheet: [~BT~hive.file~BT~](file.md); the ~BT~Table~BT~ type:
        [tables](../tables.md); running a ~BT~query~BT~: [queries](../queries.md).

        *From ~BT~spec/14-stdlib.md~BT~.*
    `)
}

// --- The small, whole modules ------------------------------------------------

func conv(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.conv~BT~

        Pure, so usable in a ~BT~func~BT~. **Hive never widens a number implicitly**; this
        module is the only way from ~BT~Int~BT~ to ~BT~Float~BT~.

        | call | signature |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~ceil(v)~BT~ ~BT~floor(v)~BT~ ~BT~round(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Float): Int~BT~ |
        | ~BT~itf(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Float~BT~ |
        | ~BT~itf32(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Float32~BT~ — the nearest one |
        | ~BT~its(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~fts(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Float): Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~sti(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): Result<Int, ConversionError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~stf(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): Result<Float, ConversionError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~bytesOf(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): UInt8[dyn]~BT~ — the bytes it holds |
        | ~BT~fromBytes(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~(UInt8[]): Str~BT~ — never fails: a ~BT~Str~BT~ holds any bytes |

        - ~BT~round~BT~ rounds **half away from zero**.
        - A ~BT~ConversionError~BT~ carries the offending ~BT~input~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~.
        - Converting ~BT~+Inf~BT~, ~BT~-Inf~BT~, ~BT~NaN~BT~ or a too-large ~BT~Float~BT~ to an ~BT~Int~BT~ is the
          language's **one unspecified case**. Non-finite values come from ~BT~Float~BT~
          overflow and ~BT~stf("Inf")~BT~: check the range first if it matters.
        - Interpolation needs none of this: ~BT~"loaded \{n} of \{total}"~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.conv.sti(field) is Result.Ok(n) \{
        	echo n * 2
        }
        echo hive.math.max(hive.conv.itf(0), health)   // ~BT~0~BT~ alone would not compile
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func math(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.math~BT~

        | | |
        | --- | --- |
        | **Constants** | ~BT~pi()~BT~ |
        | **Angles** | ~BT~sin~BT~ ~BT~cos~BT~ ~BT~tan~BT~ ~BT~asin~BT~ ~BT~acos~BT~ ~BT~atan2(y, x)~BT~ |
        | **Distances** | ~BT~sqrt~BT~ ~BT~hypot(x, y)~BT~ ~BT~abs~BT~ |
        | **Bounds** | ~BT~min(a, b)~BT~ ~BT~max(a, b)~BT~ ~BT~clamp(value, low, high)~BT~ |

        **All take and answer ~BT~Float~BT~; there are no integer versions.** Given only
        ~BT~Float32~BT~s, a call answers a ~BT~Float32~BT~. ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ is a
        **compile error** (~BT~0~BT~ is an ~BT~Int~BT~): convert with [~BT~hive.conv.itf~BT~](conv.md).
        Clamp an ~BT~Int~BT~ with an ~BT~if~BT~.

        - No errors: outside a function's domain the answer is the non-finite ~BT~Float~BT~
          the arithmetic produces.
        - ~BT~atan2~BT~ takes both legs, not their ratio, so it tells the four quadrants apart.
        - ~BT~clamp~BT~ is exactly ~BT~min(max(v, low), high)~BT~; reversed bounds answer ~BT~high~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        angle := hive.math.atan2(dy, dx)
        reach := hive.math.hypot(dx, dy)
        held  := hive.math.clamp(wanted, 0.0, 1.0)
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func maps(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.map~BT~

        A dictionary. It is a module; there is no map literal.

        | call | type | what it does |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~new()~BT~ | ~BT~Map<K, T>~BT~ | the empty map |
        | ~BT~set(m, key, value)~BT~ | ~BT~(mut Map<K, T>, K, T): void~BT~ | adds or replaces a pair |
        | ~BT~get(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K): Result<T, Bool>~BT~ | the value, or ~BT~Error(false)~BT~ |
        | ~BT~has(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K): Bool~BT~ | the same question without the value |
        | ~BT~delete(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(mut Map<K, T>, K): void~BT~ | removes a pair |
        | ~BT~plus(m, key, value)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K, T): Map<K, T>~BT~ | a new map with the pair added or replaced |
        | ~BT~minus(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K): Map<K, T>~BT~ | a new map without the pair |
        | ~BT~keys(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>): K[dyn]~BT~ | its keys, in order |
        | ~BT~values(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>): T[dyn]~BT~ | its values, in the same order |
        | ~BT~fromTable(t)~BT~ | ~BT~(Table): Map<Str, Str>~BT~ | reads rows as key/value pairs |
        | ~BT~toTable(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<Str, Str>): Table~BT~ | one two-cell row per pair |

        ~CODE~hive
        mut hive.map.Map<Str, Int> counts = hive.map.new()
        hive.map.set(counts, "apple", 3)

        for each key in hive.map.keys(counts) \{
        	if hive.map.get(counts, key) is Result.Ok(n) \{
        		echo "\{key}: \{n}"
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## The rules

        - **~BT~new()~BT~ takes its type from where it lands** (a declaration, a ~BT~return~BT~ of
          a callable declaring a map, a parameter or field declared as one) or, bound
          with ~BT~:=~BT~, from its **first later use**: after ~BT~hive.map.set(m, "a", 1)~BT~, ~BT~m~BT~
          is a ~BT~Map<Str, Int>~BT~ for good, and ~BT~set~BT~ of a ~BT~Str~BT~ value is a compile error.
        - **Keys** are compared and hashed whole: ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~, ~BT~Atom~BT~,
          or a declared type whose every field is one of those. **Storage is never a
          key**: ~BT~Map<Str[dyn], Int>~BT~ is a compile error.
        - **No access by position**: ~BT~m[0]~BT~ (the error points at ~BT~get~BT~) and ~BT~for each~BT~
          over a map are compile errors. Loop over ~BT~keys(m)~BT~.
        - **Order is the order keys were set in**, the same every run, for ~BT~keys~BT~,
          ~BT~values~BT~, ~BT~toTable~BT~ and ~BT~echo~BT~. Replacing a value keeps its key's place.
        - **Writing needs ~BT~mut~BT~**, by ~BT~append~BT~'s rule; a ~BT~proc~BT~ may take an
          ~BT~m: mut hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~ parameter.
        - **~BT~plus~BT~ and ~BT~minus~BT~ leave the map passed in unchanged**, so it needs no
          ~BT~mut~BT~: ~BT~next := hive.map.plus(counts, "fig", 1)~BT~. A key ~BT~plus~BT~ replaces keeps
          its place.
        - **A map is a value**; ~BT~keys~BT~ and ~BT~values~BT~ answer fresh vectors.
        - **~BT~==~BT~ compares the pairs**, ignoring order. Maps have no ordering between
          them, so ~BT~sort~BT~ on a vector of maps, or of a type holding one, is a compile
          error.
        - **A map does not encode or travel**: ~BT~encode~BT~ and a
          [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](syslink.md) mailbox refuse it. Send the pairs as a vector of
          a declared type. Exception: a [~BT~JsonValue~BT~](json.md) object's properties.
        - **The ~BT~map(v, f)~BT~ builtin coexists** with the module: ~BT~map.keys(m)~BT~ is the
          module, a bare ~BT~map(...)~BT~ call the builtin.
    `)
}

func files(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.file~BT~

        Contents are ~BT~Str~BT~, which holds bytes, so **a binary file survives a read/write
        round trip**. Also reads a [~BT~Table~BT~](../tables.md) from ~BT~csv~BT~, ~BT~xlsx~BT~ or ~BT~ods~BT~.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~read(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~lines(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~write(path, contents)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ — the bytes written |
        | ~BT~append(path, contents)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~size(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~copy(from, to)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~delete(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~makeDir(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~move(from, to)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~list(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], FileError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~exists(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — **not** a ~BT~Result~BT~; a directory counts |
        | ~BT~isDir(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — whether it is a directory |
        | ~BT~workingDirectory()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ — absolute |
        | ~BT~home()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ — the user's home folder |
        | ~BT~executable()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ — the running program's file |
        | ~BT~asset(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ — a file from ~BT~assets/~BT~, out of the executable |
        | ~BT~csv(path, separator)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, TableError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~xlsx(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], TableError>~BT~ — one per sheet |
        | ~BT~ods(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], TableError>~BT~ — one per table |

        A ~BT~FileError~BT~ is ~BT~NotFound~BT~, ~BT~Permission~BT~, ~BT~Exists~BT~ or ~BT~Io~BT~, each with the
        ~BT~path~BT~ and the underlying ~BT~message~BT~. A file that opens but does not hold what it
        claims gives a ~BT~TableError~BT~ (~BT~path~BT~, ~BT~message~BT~).

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.file.read("./notes.txt") is Result.Ok(text) \{
        	echo text
        }

        if hive.file.write(path, rendered) is Result.Error(why) \{
        	echo "cannot write \{path}: \{why.message}"
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Behaviour

        - ~BT~lines~BT~ drops **the empty piece after a trailing newline** and any Windows
          carriage returns.
        - ~BT~write~BT~ replaces or creates; ~BT~append~BT~ adds to the end. **Neither creates
          parent directories**: ~BT~makeDir~BT~ first.
        - ~BT~makeDir~BT~ creates **missing parents too**; an existing directory is not an
          error.
        - ~BT~delete~BT~ removes a file or an **empty** directory.
        - ~BT~list~BT~ answers bare names, **sorted**.
        - ~BT~writeSecret(path, contents)~BT~ writes a [~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~](../types.md#secret)
          **readable only by the user running the program**: mode ~BT~0600~BT~ on Linux and
          macOS; on Windows an access list naming that account, protected so the folder
          hands nothing down. Access is set **before any of the secret is written**; an
          existing file is narrowed, not trusted; one owned by somebody else is an
          ~BT~Error~BT~. **Not encryption**: an administrator, and ~BT~root~BT~, can read any file.
        - Tests run in the generated project's directory, not the entrypoint's folder:
          **a test that needs files writes them itself** (~BT~makeDir~BT~, ~BT~write~BT~). See
          [testing](../testing.md).

        ## CSV

        - **The separator is required** (Hive has no optional parameters): one
          character; a longer one is an error.
        - Ragged rows stay ragged, blank lines are skipped, a leading byte-order mark is
          dropped.
        - Only the readers a program names are linked: CSV pulls in neither
          ~BT~archive/zip~BT~ nor ~BT~encoding/xml~BT~.

        ## Where the program is

        ~BT~workingDirectory()~BT~, ~BT~home()~BT~ and ~BT~executable()~BT~ answer an **absolute path with
        ~BT~/~BT~ separators on every platform** (the form every call here takes, Windows
        included), or ~BT~NotFound~BT~ where the system cannot say. Under ~BT~hive run~BT~,
        ~BT~workingDirectory()~BT~ is the entrypoint's folder and ~BT~executable()~BT~ the copy the
        compiler built.

        ## What the program carries

        **~BT~assets/~BT~ beside the entrypoint is built into the executable** when the
        program reads it with ~BT~asset~BT~ (as for a window's typeface and a scene's
        recordings), so the executable needs nothing beside it:

        ~CODE~hive
        // assets/logo.png, beside this file
        if hive.file.asset("logo.png") is Result.Ok(data) \{
        	if hive.image.decode(data) is Result.Ok(logo) \{
        		echo "\{logo.width} by \{logo.height}"
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        Paths are relative to ~BT~assets/~BT~. A path the build put nothing at is ~BT~NotFound~BT~,
        as is every path in a program built with no ~BT~assets/~BT~. Names starting with ~BT~.~BT~
        or ~BT~_~BT~ are left out.
    `)
}

func term(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.term~BT~

        Terminal I/O. Running another program: [term-exec](term-exec.md).

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~print(text)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — the text to **stdout**, ending no line |
        | ~BT~println(text)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — a line to **stdout** |
        | ~BT~status(text)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — a line to **stderr** |
        | ~BT~isTerminal()~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
        | ~BT~platform()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — ~BT~goos/goarch~BT~, e.g. ~BT~linux/arm64~BT~ |
        | ~BT~read()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~readSecret()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, SecretError>~BT~ — see [~BT~Secret~BT~](../types.md#secret) |
        | ~BT~readSecretBypass()~BT~ | ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~ — the same, in ordinary memory; **cannot fail** |
        | ~BT~raw(on)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, TermError>~BT~ — keys arrive one at a time, unshown |
        | ~BT~readKey()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Key, TermError>~BT~ — the next key, or a paste |
        | ~BT~size()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Size, TermError>~BT~ — the window's ~BT~width~BT~ and ~BT~height~BT~ |
        | ~BT~args()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~flags()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the arguments as one line for ~BT~codec()~BT~, quoted where they were |
        | ~BT~positional(line)~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ — the line's words that are no flag's |
        | ~BT~codec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<FlagError>~BT~ — the value that says command-line flags |
        | ~BT~exit(code)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — nothing after it runs |

        ## Output and input

        - ~BT~print~BT~ writes the text as is; ~BT~println~BT~ is ~BT~echo~BT~ for a ~BT~Str~BT~ only.
        - ~BT~status~BT~ is for progress: it stays on screen when stdout is redirected.
        - ~BT~isTerminal()~BT~: whether **stderr** is a terminal, not a file or pipe. Check it
          before reporting progress.
        - ~BT~platform()~BT~ is the **build target**, as ~BT~hive build --target~BT~ spells it
          (~BT~windows/amd64~BT~, ~BT~darwin/arm64~BT~).
        - On Windows the runtime enables escape sequences at start.
        - ~BT~read()~BT~ blocks for a line, newline stripped, **parking only the calling
          virtual thread**. At end of input it answers what preceded EOF, ~BT~""~BT~ if
          nothing.
        - ~BT~readSecret()~BT~ is ~BT~read()~BT~ with echo off, restored on return and on interrupt
          at the prompt. With no terminal it reads unhidden.
        - ~BT~readSecretBypass()~BT~ uses swappable memory (as ~BT~bypass~BT~ is to ~BT~hide~BT~), so
          there is no locked-memory limit to fail on.

        ## A key at a time

        ~BT~raw(true)~BT~: no echo, no line buffering, and **Ctrl+C arrives as a key** instead
        of ending the program. Output is unchanged: a newline still starts the next
        line. **Raw mode is restored however the
        program ends** (~BT~main~BT~ returning, a panic, ~BT~exit~BT~, the last window closing, an
        outside signal), or sooner by ~BT~raw(false)~BT~. A stdin that is not a terminal gives
        ~BT~NotATerminal~BT~. In raw mode ~BT~read()~BT~ never finishes: use ~BT~readKey()~BT~.

        ~BT~readKey()~BT~ answers a ~BT~Key~BT~ with a ~BT~name~BT~ and the ~BT~text~BT~ it types. **Names are
        ~BT~hive.ui~BT~'s** ([ui-events](ui-events.md)):

        - nothing or only Shift held: what it types — ~BT~"a"~BT~, ~BT~"A"~BT~, ~BT~":"~BT~, ~BT~"ç"~BT~;
        - Ctrl or Alt held: modifiers in the order Ctrl, Shift, Alt, then the key —
          ~BT~"Ctrl+C"~BT~, ~BT~"Shift+Alt+X"~BT~, ~BT~"Ctrl+ArrowRight"~BT~;
        - keys that type nothing: ~BT~Enter~BT~ ~BT~Escape~BT~ ~BT~Tab~BT~ ~BT~Backspace~BT~ ~BT~Delete~BT~ ~BT~Insert~BT~
          ~BT~Space~BT~ ~BT~ArrowUp~BT~ ~BT~ArrowDown~BT~ ~BT~ArrowLeft~BT~ ~BT~ArrowRight~BT~ ~BT~Home~BT~ ~BT~End~BT~ ~BT~PageUp~BT~
          ~BT~PageDown~BT~ ~BT~F1~BT~…~BT~F12~BT~.

        ~BT~text~BT~ is the character, ~BT~" "~BT~ for ~BT~Space~BT~, and ~BT~""~BT~ for a key that edits or
        moves or is held with Ctrl or Alt. **In raw mode a paste is one ~BT~Paste~BT~ key**,
        its ~BT~text~BT~ the whole paste, line breaks as ~BT~"\n"~BT~ (never ~BT~Enter~BT~). Input that is
        not a terminal is read key by key too; its end is ~BT~Closed~BT~.

        ~BT~size()~BT~ is in cells; ~BT~NotATerminal~BT~ when stdout is a file or pipe.

        A ~BT~TermError~BT~ is ~BT~NotATerminal~BT~, ~BT~Closed~BT~ or ~BT~Io~BT~, each with a ~BT~message~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        hive.term.raw(true)
        mut line := ""
        for ; true; \{
        	if hive.term.readKey() is Result.Ok(key) \{
        		if key.name == "Enter" || key.name == "Ctrl+C" \{
        			break
        		}
        		line += key.text
        		hive.term.print("\r> " + line)
        	}
        }
        hive.term.raw(false)
        ~CODE~

        ## Arguments and exit

        ~BT~args()~BT~ **excludes the program name**: ~BT~args()[0]~BT~ is the first argument.
        ~BT~exit(code)~BT~ ends the program with that
        status; nothing after it runs, so it is a statement, not a call.

        ~CODE~hive
        proc main(): void \{
        	args := hive.term.args()
        	if len(args) < 1 \{
        		hive.term.status("usage: greet <name>")
        		hive.term.exit(1)
        	}
        	if args bounds 0 \{
        		echo "hello \{args[0]}"
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Flags into a type

        ~BT~T.decode(line, hive.term.codec())~BT~ reads **command-line flags** into your type;
        [~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) writes them back. The line is split as a shell would,
        so quotes keep an argument whole. **Decode ~BT~flags()~BT~**, which quotes each word
        holding a space, quote or backslash; joining ~BT~args()~BT~ with spaces would split
        quoted arguments again.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Options \{
        	name: Str
        	dryRun: Bool -- Flag as dry-run n; Default as false;
        	count: Int -- Flag as count c; Default as 10;
        }

        line := hive.term.flags()
        options := Options.decode(line, hive.term.codec())
        if options is Result.Error(why) \{
        	hive.term.status("\{why.path}: expected \{why.expected}, found \{why.found}")
        	hive.term.exit(2)
        }
        words := hive.term.positional(line)

        Options.decode("--name Ada -n -c=3", hive.term.codec())
        encode(Options("Ada Lovelace", true, 3), hive.term.codec())
        // --name "Ada Lovelace" --dry-run true --count 3
        ~CODE~

        - **~BT~--name value~BT~ or ~BT~--name=value~BT~**; the name is the field's, or its
          ~BT~Flag as~BT~ (~BT~somethingElse: Str -- Flag as something-else;~BT~ is
          ~BT~--something-else~BT~).
        - **A second ~BT~Flag as~BT~ name is the short one**, after a single dash:
          ~BT~Flag as dry-run n;~BT~ gives ~BT~--dry-run~BT~ and ~BT~-n~BT~. The shorter name is the short
          one. Compile errors: two names of one length, a third name, two fields with
          one short name. ~BT~-abc~BT~ is one flag, never ~BT~-a -b -c~BT~.
        - A lone ~BT~Bool~BT~ is ~BT~true~BT~; it **takes the next word only if that is ~BT~true~BT~ or
          ~BT~false~BT~**. Other flags take the next word unless it is a flag (a negative
          number never is); write a value starting with a dash as ~BT~--name=-x~BT~, or quote
          it.
        - A vector is its flag repeated, once per element.
        - **A missing flag is an error** unless the field has a ~BT~Default~BT~ (~BT~Bool~BT~
          included); so is a flag given twice for a field that is not a vector.
        - Quotes and a backslash keep a word together; a quoted word is never a flag.
        - Only a type without variants travels as flags, each field a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~,
          ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~, or a vector of one. A failure is a
          ~BT~hive.term.FlagError~BT~ whose ~BT~path~BT~ is the flag as written (~BT~--count~BT~, ~BT~-c~BT~).

        ## The program's own words

        **Words before the first flag and after a lone ~BT~--~BT~ are the program's own**:
        ~BT~positional(line)~BT~ answers them in order; ~BT~decode~BT~ skips them. Between them,
        **an undeclared flag is an error** (listing the declared ones), as is a word
        that is no flag's value:

        ~CODE~
        lodemaria what is this               positional: what, is, this
        lodemaria -m llama -- what is this   positional: what, is, this
        lodemaria -m llama what is this      $: expected a flag (words go before the flags or after --), found "what"
        lodemaria --modle llama              --modle: expected --model/-m, ..., found a flag nothing declares
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~positional~BT~ knows no type, so it cannot tell ~BT~--model llama~BT~ (a value) from
        ~BT~--code explain~BT~ (a ~BT~Bool~BT~, then a word): words after the flags need ~BT~--~BT~. A
        line with a quote left open has no words.
    `)
}

func image(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.image~BT~

        Decodes and resizes pictures. No I/O: the bytes come from ~BT~hive.file.read~BT~,
        [~BT~hive.file.asset~BT~](file.md#what-the-program-carries) or a response's body.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~decode(data)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Picture, ImageError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~scale(picture, width, height)~BT~ | ~BT~Picture~BT~ — exactly that size |

        ~CODE~hive
        hive.image.Picture(width, height, pixels)     // Int, Int, UInt8[dyn]
        ~CODE~

        - ~BT~decode~BT~ reads **PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP**, lossy or lossless, with or without
          alpha; an animated GIF or WebP gives its first frame.
        - ~BT~pixels~BT~ is **red, green, blue, alpha (not premultiplied), four bytes a pixel,
          row by row from the top left**: pixel ~BT~(x, y)~BT~ starts at
          ~BT~(y * width + x) * 4~BT~. Indexes need a range proof:

        ~CODE~hive
        red := (y * picture.width + x) * 4
        alpha := red + 3
        pixels := picture.pixels
        if pixels bounds red && pixels bounds alpha \{
        	echo "\{pixels[red]} red at \{pixels[alpha]} alpha"
        }
        ~CODE~

        - An ~BT~ImageError~BT~ carries a ~BT~message~BT~: data that is not such a file, is cut
          short or broken, or **claims more than 100 million pixels** (refused before
          decoding).
        - ~BT~scale~BT~ makes each pixel the **average of those it covers, weighted by
          opacity**: shrinking keeps colours and an edge against transparency does not
          darken; growing repeats pixels. A size of nought is a picture of nothing.
          Keeping proportions is the caller's arithmetic:

        ~CODE~hive
        smaller := hive.image.scale(picture, 40, picture.height * 40 / picture.width)
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~examples/18-pictures~BT~ reads Hive's logo out of the executable, scales it and
        draws it in characters.
    `)
}

func termExec(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.term~BT~ — running another program

        ~BT~exec~BT~, ~BT~attach~BT~ and ~BT~execWith~BT~ wait for the command to end; the rest
        [run one alongside](#a-program-that-runs-alongside).

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~exec(command, arguments, directory)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ExecError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~attach(command, arguments, directory)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the exit status |
        | ~BT~execWith(command, arguments, directory, environment)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ExecError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~start(command, arguments, directory)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Process, ExecError>~BT~ — at once |
        | ~BT~startWith(command, arguments, directory, environment)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Process, ExecError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~processReceiveLine(process)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ProcessError>~BT~ — without the newline |
        | ~BT~processReceive(process, bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ProcessError>~BT~ — at most that many |
        | ~BT~processSend(process, text)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, ProcessError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~processCloseInput(process)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
        | ~BT~processWait(process)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the exit status |
        | ~BT~processKill(process)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — once it has ended |

        ~BT~arguments~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[]~BT~; ~BT~directory~BT~ a ~BT~Str~BT~, empty for this program's
        directory; ~BT~environment~BT~ a ~BT~hive.map.Map<Str, Str>~BT~.

        **No shell is involved**: arguments are a vector, so nothing in one is read as
        shell syntax. To use a shell, run one by name.

        ## ~BT~exec~BT~ captures, ~BT~attach~BT~ hands the terminal over

        ~BT~exec~BT~ answers **everything the command wrote**, stdout and stderr together in
        write order. **A non-zero exit status is an ~BT~Error~BT~**: an ~BT~ExecError~BT~ with
        ~BT~command~BT~, ~BT~code~BT~, ~BT~output~BT~ and ~BT~message~BT~; ~BT~code~BT~ is ~BT~-1~BT~ if the command could
        not start.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.term.exec("go", ["build", "."], buildDirectory) is Result.Error(why) \{
        	echo "the build failed:\n\{why.output}"
        	hive.term.exit(1)
        }
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~attach~BT~ gives the command **this program's terminal** (input, output, errors);
        nothing is captured.

        ## ~BT~execWith~BT~ is an overlay, never a replacement

        The pairs are set **on top of** this program's environment; a name already
        there is replaced, not duplicated. **An empty map is exactly ~BT~exec~BT~.**

        ~CODE~hive
        mut hive.map.Map<Str, Str> asked = hive.map.new()
        hive.map.set(asked, "GOOS", "linux")
        hive.map.set(asked, "GOARCH", "arm64")

        // ~BT~go env GOOS~BT~ now says ~BT~linux~BT~, and ~BT~go~BT~ is still found on the PATH.
        hive.term.execWith("go", ["build", "."], buildDirectory, asked)
        ~CODE~

        ## A program that runs alongside

        A ~BT~Process~BT~ has one field, ~BT~id~BT~, the system's number for it.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.term.start("go", ["test", "./..."], directory) is Result.Ok(tests) \{
        	for ; true; \{
        		if hive.term.processReceiveLine(tests) is Result.Ok(line) \{
        			echo line
        		} else \{
        			break
        		}
        	}
        	echo "status \{hive.term.processWait(tests)}"
        }
        ~CODE~

        - **Output and errors are one stream**, in write order. A receive blocks only
          the calling virtual thread; a last line with no newline still counts.
        - ~BT~processCloseInput~BT~ ends its input, for a command that reads to the end
          (~BT~sort~BT~, ~BT~wc~BT~).
        - ~BT~processWait~BT~ takes nothing from the process, so
          ~BT~processWait(p) with timeout 500~BT~ loses nothing.
        - **~BT~processKill~BT~ stops it and everything it started**: on Unix it leads its own
          process group, asked to end and a moment later made to; on Windows it is in
          its own job, ended whole. Its status is then ~BT~-1~BT~, and nothing more is read.
        - A ~BT~ProcessError~BT~ is ~BT~Closed~BT~ (output ended, process stopped, or input closed
          or unread), ~BT~Send~BT~ or ~BT~Receive~BT~, each with a ~BT~message~BT~. Failing to start is an
          ~BT~ExecError~BT~ with ~BT~code~BT~ ~BT~-1~BT~.
        - **A started program is stopped when its starter ends** (~BT~main~BT~ returning, a
          panic, ~BT~hive.term.exit~BT~, the last window closing, an interrupt), on Windows
          even when the starter is killed.
        - ~BT~hive.term.Process(id)~BT~ builds one with no program behind it, for a test.

        ~BT~examples/19-programs-alongside~BT~ starts itself, talks to itself and stops itself.
    `)
}

func env(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.env~BT~

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.env.get("DATABASE_URL") is Result.Ok(url) \{
        	echo url
        }
        ~CODE~

        - ~BT~get(name)~BT~ → ~BT~Result<Str, hive.env.EnvironmentError>~BT~, the error carrying
          ~BT~key~BT~ and ~BT~message~BT~.
        - ~BT~getSecret(name)~BT~: the same lookup, answering a
          [~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~](../types.md#secret), locked and unprintable in the program
          (the environment and ~BT~.env~BT~ still hold it as text).

        **Lookup order:**

        1. ~BT~.env~BT~ beside the executable;
        2. ~BT~.env~BT~ in the working directory (the entrypoint's folder under ~BT~hive run~BT~);
        3. ~BT~.env~BT~ in the parent folder of either;
        4. the OS environment.

        So a nearby ~BT~.env~BT~ wins over a shell export.

        ~BT~.env~BT~ is read **once**, on the first ~BT~get~BT~: blank lines and ~BT~#~BT~ comments are
        ignored, an ~BT~export ~BT~ prefix is allowed, quotes around a value are stripped.

        ~CODE~
        # a comment
        export DATABASE_URL="postgres://localhost/hive"
        PORT=8080
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~docker run -e~BT~ variables are read the same way; keep a private ~BT~.env~BT~ in
        ~BT~.dockerignore~BT~.
    `)
}

func task(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.task~BT~

        ## ~BT~sleep(ms)~BT~

        Parks **only the calling virtual thread**, so sleeps awaited together overlap.
        A non-positive ~BT~ms~BT~ returns at once.

        ~CODE~hive
        await [slowly("a"), slowly("b")]   // one second, not two
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~sleepForever()~BT~

        Parks the calling virtual thread for good, for a ~BT~main~BT~ with nothing left to do
        itself. The program still ends at ~BT~hive.term.exit~BT~ or when its last window
        closes.

        ## ~BT~TimeoutError~BT~

        What ~BT~with timeout~BT~ answers with: ~BT~waited~BT~ (an ~BT~Int~BT~, the milliseconds asked
        for) and a ~BT~message~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        if slowShout("worth waiting for") with timeout 100 is Result.Error(err) \{
        	echo "gave up after \{err.waited}ms"
        }
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~with timeout~BT~ is a language clause ([concurrency](../concurrency.md)). It
        follows anything that waits, makes the result
        ~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~, is **one deadline across a whole
        await-all**, and abandons **the waiting, not the work**: the call runs on and
        only its result is dropped.
    `)
}

func times(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.time~BT~

        **Times are plain ~BT~Int~BT~s: Unix milliseconds.** No date or duration types; time
        arithmetic is ~BT~Int~BT~ arithmetic.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~now()~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ |
        | ~BT~timezone()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the zone name or abbreviation at this instant |
        | ~BT~timezoneOffset()~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — minutes east of UTC |
        | ~BT~format(time, template)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — rendered in **local** time |
        | ~BT~dateFrom(year, month, day)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — that date at midnight |
        | ~BT~dateTimeFrom(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — that instant |

        ~BT~format~BT~ takes a ~BT~strftime~BT~-style template; unrecognised ~BT~%x~BT~ escapes pass
        through verbatim:

        | | | | |
        |---|---|---|---|
        | ~BT~%Y~BT~ year (4) | ~BT~%y~BT~ year (2) | ~BT~%m~BT~ month | ~BT~%d~BT~ day |
        | ~BT~%H~BT~ hour 00–23 | ~BT~%I~BT~ hour 01–12 | ~BT~%M~BT~ minute | ~BT~%S~BT~ second |
        | ~BT~%p~BT~ AM/PM | ~BT~%j~BT~ day-of-year | ~BT~%Z~BT~ zone name | ~BT~%z~BT~ zone offset |
        | ~BT~%A~BT~/~BT~%a~BT~ weekday | ~BT~%B~BT~/~BT~%b~BT~ month name | ~BT~%%~BT~ literal ~BT~%~BT~ | |

        ~CODE~hive
        echo hive.time.format(hive.time.now(), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
        echo hive.time.format(stamp, "%A %e %B")           // %e passes through
        elapsed := hive.time.now() - started               // milliseconds, as an Int
        ~CODE~

        ## Building a time

        ~BT~dateFrom~BT~ and ~BT~dateTimeFrom~BT~ build in **local** time, so a stamp round-trips
        with ~BT~format~BT~. **A field out of range carries into the next**: month 13 is next
        January, day 0 the previous month's last day.

        ~CODE~hive
        hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 9, 14)                  // 2026-09-14 00:00:00
        hive.time.dateTimeFrom(2026, 9, 14, 13, 30, 0)   // 2026-09-14 13:30:00
        hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 13, 1)                  // 2027-01-01
        hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 3, 0)                   // 2026-02-28, February's last
        ~CODE~

        ## The current instant, field by field

        Fields of **now**, in local time; no arguments. For another stamp's fields, use
        ~BT~format~BT~.

        | call | answers |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~year()~BT~ | the full year — ~BT~2026~BT~ |
        | ~BT~month()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~–~BT~12~BT~ |
        | ~BT~day()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~–~BT~31~BT~ |
        | ~BT~weekday()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~ Monday – ~BT~7~BT~ Sunday, the ISO numbering |
        | ~BT~lastDayOfMonth()~BT~ | ~BT~28~BT~–~BT~31~BT~ — how long **this** month is |
        | ~BT~hour()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~23~BT~ |
        | ~BT~minute()~BT~, ~BT~second()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~59~BT~ |
        | ~BT~millisecond()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~999~BT~ |

        ~CODE~hive
        year := hive.time.year()
        month := hive.time.month()
        first := hive.time.dateFrom(year, month, 1)
        last := hive.time.dateFrom(year, month, hive.time.lastDayOfMonth())
        ~CODE~

        Stamps, ~BT~with timeout~BT~ and [~BT~hive.task.sleep~BT~](task.md) all use milliseconds.
        ~BT~millisecond()~BT~ is now's sub-second **field**, not a stamp.
    `)
}

func json(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.json~BT~

        **Your type declarations are the JSON schema**: no schema, field tags or
        reflection. Decoders are derived at compile time.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~hive.json.codec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<JsonError>~BT~ — the value that says JSON |
        | ~BT~T.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~ — ~BT~E~BT~ is the codec's error, ~BT~JsonError~BT~ here |
        | ~BT~T.Variant.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | the same — one variant's payload |
        | ~BT~encode(value, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — **cannot fail** |
        | ~BT~JsonValue.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<JsonValue, _>~BT~ — any document |

        A ~BT~JsonError~BT~ carries the failing ~BT~path~BT~, what was ~BT~expected~BT~ and what was
        ~BT~found~BT~: ~BT~Mismatch~BT~ for the wrong shape, ~BT~Broken~BT~ for a value breaking one of its
        type's [laws](../laws.md#a-types-laws).

        **Only the codec names the format**, so other formats are other codecs:
        [~BT~jwtCodec~BT~](crypto-jwt.md), [~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~](net-urls.md),
        [~BT~hive.term.codec~BT~](term.md). ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<E>~BT~ names its decoding error,
        owned by its module. A codec is an **ordinary value**: stored or passed, it
        works as one written at the call.

        ~CODE~hive
        type User \{
        	id:   Int
        	name: Str
        }

        if User.decode(body, hive.json.codec()) is Result.Ok(user) \{
        	echo user.name
        }
        echo encode(user, hive.json.codec())
        ~CODE~

        ## The annotations steer both codecs

        ~BT~--~BT~ annotations set a field's key and what happens when it is absent
        ([declarations](../declarations.md#field-annotations)):

        ~CODE~hive
        type User \{
        	name: Str -- JSON as username;
        	age:  Int
        	firstName: Str -- Default as "";
        }

        encode(User("ada", 36, "Ada"), hive.json.codec())
        // \{"username":"ada","age":36,"firstName":"Ada"}

        User.decode("\\{\"username\":\"ada\",\"age\":36}", hive.json.codec())
        // Result.Ok — ~BT~firstName~BT~ fell back to ""
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~JSON as~BT~ renames in **both** directions. ~BT~Default as~BT~ makes a field optional:
        an absent key and a ~BT~null~BT~ both read as the literal.

        ## Decoding and encoding

        - **Missing fields, wrong types and wrong static vector lengths are errors** at
          the failing path. A ~BT~Default~BT~ covers a missing field, **not** a wrong type.
        - **JSON fields the type doesn't declare are ignored.**
        - **Variants are ~BT~\{"VariantName": \{...}}~BT~**; JSON ~BT~null~BT~ selects a type's first
          field-less variant.
        - ~BT~encode~BT~ works from the **static** type, so it cannot fail.
        - **A map does not encode** (decoding is by declared shape): send its pairs as a
          vector of a declared type, or use a ~BT~JsonValue~BT~, whose objects are maps.
        - **Nor does a ~BT~Table~BT~**, anywhere in a value. ~BT~Table.decode~BT~ does not exist.

        ## When the shape is not yours to declare

        ~BT~hive.json.JsonValue~BT~ is any document:

        ~CODE~hive
        type JsonValue \{
        	String \{ value: Str }
        	Boolean \{ value: Bool }
        	Int \{ value: Int }
        	Float \{ value: Float }
        	Array \{ values: JsonValue[dyn] }
        	Object \{ properties: hive.map.Map<Str, JsonValue> }
        	Null
        }
        ~CODE~

        It reads and writes plain JSON (~BT~"x"~BT~, never ~BT~\{"String": \{"value": "x"}}~BT~) and
        is matched like any union:

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.json.JsonValue.decode(config, hive.json.codec()) is Result.Ok(doc) \{
        	if doc is hive.json.JsonValue.Object(properties) \{
        		for each key in hive.map.keys(properties) \{
        			echo key
        		}
        		if hive.map.get(properties, "name") is Result.Ok(name) \{
        			echo name
        		}
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        - A number is an ~BT~Int~BT~ where it reads as one, else a ~BT~Float~BT~; ~BT~1.0~BT~ stays a
          ~BT~Float~BT~ both ways.
        - An object's properties are a [map](map.md) in document order. A key written
          twice keeps its first place and its last value, as for a declared type. Text
          after the document is an error.
        - Holding maps, a ~BT~JsonValue~BT~ has no order: ~BT~sort~BT~ takes a ~BT~func~BT~ saying which
          comes first.
        - ~BT~null~BT~ is ~BT~Null()~BT~ wherever it appears.
        - It is read whole: ~BT~JsonValue.String.decode~BT~ is refused.
        - A field may be a ~BT~JsonValue~BT~, leaving that part of a declared type undeclared.

        [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](syslink.md) messages cross the wire as these same codecs.
    `)
}

// --- hive.crypto -------------------------------------------------------------

func crypto(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — hashing, encoding, random

        Pure, so usable in a ~BT~func~BT~. Encryption: [crypto-encrypt](crypto-encrypt.md);
        tokens: [crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md).

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~sha256(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
        | ~BT~sha512(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
        | ~BT~hmacSha256(input, key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
        | ~BT~base64Encode(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~base64Decode(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, CryptoError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~randomHex(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — ~BT~bytes~BT~ random bytes, as hex |
        | ~BT~randomSecret(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, SecretError>~BT~ — the same, never a ~BT~Str~BT~ |

        - Fallible calls answer ~BT~Result<_, hive.crypto.CryptoError>~BT~: ~BT~Malformed~BT~,
          ~BT~BadSignature~BT~, or ~BT~LimitExceeded~BT~ or ~BT~Unsupported~BT~ where a ~BT~Secret~BT~ could not
          be locked, each with a ~BT~message~BT~.
        - **Only ~BT~base64Decode~BT~ and ~BT~randomSecret~BT~ can fail here**: hashing cannot, and
          ~BT~randomHex~BT~ draws from the platform's own source.
        - A key is a ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~; everything else is a ~BT~Str~BT~, which holds bytes, so
          hashing a file read with [~BT~hive.file.read~BT~](file.md) hashes the file.

        ~CODE~hive
        echo hive.crypto.sha256("the message")
        echo hive.crypto.hmacSha256(payload, bypass("a literal key"))
        token := hive.crypto.randomHex(16)          // 32 hex characters

        if hive.crypto.base64Decode(field) is Result.Ok(raw) \{
        	echo len(raw)
        }
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func cryptoEncrypt(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — encryption

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~encrypt(plaintext: Secret<Str>, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — base64; **cannot fail** |
        | ~BT~decrypt(ciphertext, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, CryptoError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~decryptBypass(ciphertext, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, CryptoError>~BT~ — the same, in ordinary memory |

        Plaintext, password and what ~BT~decrypt~BT~ opens are
        [~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~s](../types.md#secret): the plaintext goes from locked memory into
        AES-GCM and back, never a ~BT~Str~BT~. The ciphertext is a ~BT~Str~BT~, safe to print and
        store.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.term.readSecret() is Result.Ok(password) \{
        	sealed := hive.crypto.encrypt(bypass("attack at dawn"), password)

        	if hive.crypto.decrypt(sealed, password) is Result.Ok(opened) \{
        		echo reveal(opened)
        	} else if hive.crypto.decrypt(sealed, password) is Result.Error(crypto.CryptoError.BadSignature(_)) \{
        		echo "wrong password, or the message was changed"
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ~BT~decrypt~BT~ fails as ~BT~LimitExceeded~BT~ or ~BT~Unsupported~BT~ when there is no locked
        memory to open into. ~BT~decryptBypass~BT~ uses ordinary, swappable memory (as
        ~BT~bypass~BT~ is to ~BT~hide~BT~), so it fails only as ~BT~Malformed~BT~ or ~BT~BadSignature~BT~.

        ## What it does, exactly

        - **AES-256-GCM**, base64-encoded; key derived with **600,000 rounds of
          PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256** over a random salt.
        - **Fresh salt and nonce every call**: the same text under the same password
          never encrypts alike.
        - GCM's tag travels with the ciphertext, so **an edited message is rejected** as
          ~BT~"BadSignature"~BT~ — also what a **wrong password** gives. The two are
          indistinguishable by design; do not tell a user which it was.
        - **Slow on purpose** (the rounds make it safe for a typed password): derive
          once and hold the result; don't encrypt field by field in a loop.

        **Integrity without secrecy** (proving a message was not altered, by someone who
        has the key): ~BT~hmacSha256~BT~ on [crypto](crypto.md). **A claim somebody else has
        to read**: [crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md).
    `)
}

func cryptoJwt(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — JWT

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~jwtCodec(secret: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<JwtError>~BT~ — HS256 under this secret |
        | ~BT~jwtDecode(token)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, CryptoError>~BT~ — **without verifying** |
        | ~BT~jwtHeader(token)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<hive.crypto.Header, CryptoError>~BT~ |

        There is no sign or verify call: ~BT~jwtCodec~BT~ is a codec like
        [~BT~hive.json.codec~BT~](json.md), used with [~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) and
        ~BT~T.decode~BT~. The claims are **your own type**.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Claims \{
        	sub: Str
        	exp: Int
        }

        token := encode(Claims("ada", hive.time.now() / 1000 + 3600), hive.crypto.jwtCodec(secret))

        if Claims.decode(token, hive.crypto.jwtCodec(secret)) is Result.Ok(claims) \{
        	echo claims.sub
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## What the codec checks

        - The signature **and the ~BT~exp~BT~/~BT~nbf~BT~ claims**, before reading anything.
        - **Only HS256 is accepted**: ~BT~alg: none~BT~ and algorithm confusion are rejected
          outright.
        - A failed check is a ~BT~hive.crypto.JwtError~BT~, like claims of the wrong shape;
          its ~BT~found~BT~ opens with ~BT~"Expired"~BT~, ~BT~"BadSignature"~BT~, ~BT~"AlgorithmMismatch"~BT~ or
          ~BT~"Malformed"~BT~.
        - ~BT~exp~BT~ and ~BT~nbf~BT~ are Unix **seconds** (RFC 7519); [~BT~hive.time.now()~BT~](time.md)
          is milliseconds, so use ~BT~hive.time.now() / 1000~BT~.

        ~BT~jwtDecode~BT~ **verifies nothing**: it is for inspection only, such as logging
        claims or reading ~BT~sub~BT~ to pick the secret to check with. **Never authorise on
        what it answers.**

        ~BT~jwtHeader~BT~ reads ~BT~alg~BT~, ~BT~typ~BT~ and ~BT~kid~BT~, so a program rotating keys can pick
        the secret.
    `)
}

// --- hive.net ----------------------------------------------------------------

func net(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~

        HTTP, WebSockets and raw TCP, clients and servers. All of it performs I/O
        (usable in a ~BT~func~BT~ or a ~BT~proc~BT~); **none of it adds a dependency**.

        | page | what is on it |
        | --- | --- |
        | [net-http](net-http.md) | ~BT~httpRequest~BT~, ~BT~httpOpen~BT~ for a body read as it arrives, ~BT~httpServe~BT~, and the types |
        | [net-websockets](net-websockets.md) | ~BT~wsConnect~BT~, ~BT~wsServe~BT~, ~BT~wsSend~BT~, ~BT~wsReceive~BT~, and ~BT~httpWsServe~BT~ for HTTP on the same port |
        | [net-sockets](net-sockets.md) | ~BT~socketConnect~BT~, ~BT~socketServe~BT~, and the stream |
        | [net-names](net-names.md) | ~BT~resolve~BT~, ~BT~localAddress~BT~ |
        | [net-urls](net-urls.md) | ~BT~urlEncode~BT~, ~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~ |

        ## Servers

        * **~BT~httpServe~BT~, ~BT~wsServe~BT~, ~BT~socketServe~BT~ and ~BT~httpWsServe~BT~ block forever**, so
          one usually runs on its own virtual thread:
          ~BT~async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)~BT~. There is no ~BT~serve~BT~.
        * The handler runs **once per request or connection, on a thread of its own**.
          It needs no lock only because it shares nothing; long-lived shared state
          belongs in a [service](syslink.md).
        * **A handler is passed by name**; its shape is checked at compile time, also
          through a partial application, which is how it gets what it needs:

        ~CODE~hive
        proc handle(db: hive.sql.SqlConnection, req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
        	// ...
        }

        async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle(db, _))
        ~CODE~

        ## Errors

        Each is a union, every variant carrying a ~BT~message~BT~:

        | type | its variants |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~HttpError~BT~ | none — a struct carrying the ~BT~url~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~ |
        | ~BT~HttpStreamError~BT~ | ~BT~Closed~BT~ ~BT~Receive~BT~ |
        | ~BT~WsError~BT~ | ~BT~Handshake~BT~ ~BT~Protocol~BT~ ~BT~Closed~BT~ ~BT~Send~BT~ ~BT~Receive~BT~ |
        | ~BT~SocketError~BT~ | ~BT~Connect~BT~ ~BT~Closed~BT~ ~BT~Send~BT~ ~BT~Receive~BT~ |
        | ~BT~NetError~BT~ | ~BT~NotFound~BT~ ~BT~Lookup~BT~ ~BT~NoAddress~BT~ |

        A server answering with a **page rather than a string** uses ~BT~hive.ui.page~BT~,
        which renders the view tree a window paints: see [ui](ui.md).
    `)
}

func netHttp(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — HTTP

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~httpRequest(req)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<HttpResponse, HttpError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~httpOpen(req)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<HttpStream, HttpError>~BT~ — once the headers are in |
        | ~BT~httpReceiveLine(stream)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, HttpStreamError>~BT~ — without the newline |
        | ~BT~httpReceive(stream, bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, HttpStreamError>~BT~ — at most that many |
        | ~BT~httpClose(stream)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
        | ~BT~httpServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |

        ~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(HttpRequest): HttpResponse~BT~.

        ~CODE~hive
        hive.net.HttpRequest(method, url, headers, body)     // Str, Str, Table, Str
        hive.net.HttpResponse(status, headers, body)         // Int, Table, Str
        hive.net.HttpStream(status, headers)                 // Int, Table, and a body still arriving
        ~CODE~

        * **Headers are a ~BT~Table~BT~ of ~BT~[name, value]~BT~ rows**: read one with
          ~BT~row(req.headers, "content-type")~BT~.
        * An ~BT~HttpError~BT~ carries the ~BT~url~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~. An ~BT~HttpStreamError~BT~ is
          ~BT~Closed~BT~ (the body ended, or the stream was closed) or ~BT~Receive~BT~ (the
          connection broke part-way), with a ~BT~message~BT~.

        ## Client

        ~CODE~hive
        asked := hive.net.HttpRequest("GET", "https://example.com/api", [], "")

        if hive.net.httpRequest(asked) is Result.Ok(answer) \{
        	if answer.status == 200 \{
        		echo answer.body
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        **~BT~Ok~BT~ means the round trip happened**: a 404 or a 500 is an ~BT~Ok~BT~ with that
        status. An ~BT~Error~BT~ means no answer at all. Several at once are an
        [await-all](../concurrency.md):
        ~BT~both := await [hive.net.httpRequest(a), hive.net.httpRequest(b)]~BT~.

        ## A body read as it arrives

        For a body written a piece at a time (a language model's answer, a followed
        log, server-sent events), ~BT~httpOpen~BT~ answers once the **status and headers** are
        in, where ~BT~httpRequest~BT~ waits for the whole body:

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.net.httpOpen(asked) is Result.Ok(stream) \{
        	for ; true; \{
        		if hive.net.httpReceiveLine(stream) is Result.Ok(line) \{
        			echo line
        		} else \{
        			break
        		}
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        * ~BT~Ok~BT~ and ~BT~Error~BT~ mean what they do for ~BT~httpRequest~BT~; a 404's body is read the
          same way.
        * ~BT~httpReceiveLine~BT~ strips ~BT~\n~BT~ or ~BT~\r\n~BT~, and answers the last line with or
          without one. ~BT~httpReceive~BT~ answers whatever has arrived, up to ~BT~bytes~BT~. Both
          **block only the calling virtual thread**.
        * **~BT~httpClose~BT~ drops the connection**, telling the server nobody is reading:
          how a client stops a model that keeps going. Called from another thread while
          a receive waits, that receive answers ~BT~Closed~BT~. Every copy of a stream is the
          same stream.
        * **Only connecting is bounded**: headers may wait for a model to load, and a
          body may take minutes. ~BT~with timeout~BT~ on a receive gives up the waiting, not
          the receive, which still takes the next line; to do other work meanwhile, read
          on a thread of its own.
        * ~BT~hive.net.HttpStream(200, [])~BT~ has no connection behind it, for tests; reading
          it answers ~BT~Closed~BT~. ~BT~examples/21-streaming-answer~BT~ reads a stand-in model's
          answer and stops one stuck in a loop.

        ## Server

        ~CODE~hive
        proc handle(req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
        	if req.url is "/health" \{
        		return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], "ok")
        	}
        	if req.url is "/users/\{id}" \{
        		return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], "user \{id}")
        	}
        	return hive.net.HttpResponse(404, [], "not found")
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)
        }
        ~CODE~

        * Route with [string patterns](../patterns.md) on ~BT~req.url~BT~; a hole between two
          ~BT~/~BT~ never swallows a ~BT~/~BT~.
        * ~BT~handle~BT~ runs once per request, on a thread of its own. A program doing
          anything else runs ~BT~async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)~BT~.
        * WebSockets on the same port: ~BT~httpWsServe~BT~, whose handler is this one and
          answers 101 to hand a request over ([net-websockets](net-websockets.md)).
    `)
}

func netWebsockets(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — WebSockets

        RFC 6455. The runtime does the handshake, frame headers, masking, ping/pong and
        fragmentation; **a program sees whole messages as ~BT~Str~BT~**.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~wsConnect(url)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<WsConnection, WsError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~wsServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |
        | ~BT~httpWsServe(port, handler, wsHandler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever**; HTTP and WebSockets on one port |
        | ~BT~wsSend(conn, message)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, WsError>~BT~ — the bytes it carried |
        | ~BT~wsReceive(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, WsError>~BT~ — the next message |
        | ~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~HttpRequest~BT~ — the request that opened it |
        | ~BT~wsClose(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

        * ~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(WsConnection): void~BT~, run once per connection on a
          thread of its own.
        * A ~BT~WsConnection~BT~ is a handle with one field: **~BT~conn.address~BT~, the far end as
          ~BT~host:port~BT~**.
        * A ~BT~WsError~BT~ is ~BT~Handshake~BT~, ~BT~Protocol~BT~, ~BT~Closed~BT~, ~BT~Send~BT~ or ~BT~Receive~BT~. **A
          peer that hangs up is ~BT~WsError.Closed~BT~**: the ordinary end, not a failure to
          report.
        * A receive **blocks only the calling virtual thread**; a client doing other
          work reads on a thread of its own.
        * ~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~ gives a server **the path and headers it was reached at**:
          the URL a browser opened, and any token in it.

        ~CODE~hive
        proc echoBack(conn: hive.net.WsConnection): void \{
        	for ; true; \{
        		if hive.net.wsReceive(conn) is Result.Ok(message) \{
        			if hive.net.wsSend(conn, message) is Result.Error(_) \{
        				return
        			}
        		} else \{
        			return                       // "Closed": they hung up
        		}
        	}
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	hive.net.wsServe(9001, echoBack)
        }
        ~CODE~

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.net.wsConnect("wss://example.com/feed") is Result.Ok(conn) \{
        	_ := hive.net.wsSend(conn, "subscribe")
        	if hive.net.wsReceive(conn) is Result.Ok(first) \{
        		echo first
        	}
        	hive.net.wsClose(conn)
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Who is at the far end

        ~BT~conn.address~BT~ is a **field**, not a call: the client on a connection a server
        accepted, the server on one ~BT~wsConnect~BT~ opened. An IPv6 host is in brackets:

        ~CODE~hive
        func host(conn: hive.net.WsConnection): Str \{
        	switch conn.address \{
        		"[\{name}]:\{port is (\d+)}" \{ return name }
        		"\{name}:\{port is (\d+)}" \{ return name }
        		_ \{ return conn.address }
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **Behind a reverse proxy it is the proxy's address.** The client's is then in
          the header the proxy wrote (usually ~BT~X-Forwarded-For~BT~) on ~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~;
          trust it only from a proxy you put there.
        * ~BT~hive.net.WsConnection("203.0.113.9:4711")~BT~ has that address and **no socket
          behind it**: every call on it answers ~BT~Closed~BT~. Tests use it.

        ## HTTP and WebSockets on one port

        In ~BT~httpWsServe(port, handler, wsHandler)~BT~, **every request reaches ~BT~handler~BT~ as
        HTTP; answering 101 hands it to ~BT~wsHandler~BT~**, as under ~BT~wsServe~BT~:

        ~CODE~hive
        proc route(req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
        	switch req.url \{
        		"/health" \{ return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], "up") }
        		"/feed" \{ return hive.net.HttpResponse(101, [], "") }
        		_ \{ return hive.net.HttpResponse(404, [], "not found") }
        	}
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	hive.net.httpWsServe(8080, route, echoBack)
        }
        ~CODE~

        * One router decides page, WebSocket or nothing: a wrong token is a 401 from
          ~BT~route~BT~, before any upgrade.
        * **Of a 101 only the status is read**; the runtime writes the handshake, so its
          headers and body are not sent.
        * **A 101 to a request that asked for no upgrade is a 400**, as under
          ~BT~wsServe~BT~.
        * ~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~ is the request ~BT~handler~BT~ was handed.
    `)
}

func netSockets(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — raw TCP

        **A stream, not a queue of messages**: two sends may arrive as one read, and
        one send as two.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~socketConnect(host, port)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SocketConnection, SocketError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~socketServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |
        | ~BT~socketSend(conn, data)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, SocketError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~socketReceive(conn, bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SocketError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~socketReceiveLine(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SocketError>~BT~ — without the newline |
        | ~BT~socketPeer(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the far end's address |
        | ~BT~socketClose(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

        * ~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(SocketConnection): void~BT~, run once per connection on a
          thread of its own.
        * A ~BT~SocketConnection~BT~ is **opaque**. A ~BT~SocketError~BT~ is ~BT~Connect~BT~, ~BT~Closed~BT~,
          ~BT~Send~BT~ or ~BT~Receive~BT~.
        * **~BT~socketReceive(conn, 4096)~BT~ answers whatever had arrived, up to 4096
          bytes.** A short read is normal, so a program that needs a whole thing loops,
          usually with ~BT~socketReceiveLine~BT~: a line is a boundary the sender wrote.

        ~CODE~hive
        proc serve(conn: hive.net.SocketConnection): void \{
        	echo "connection from \{hive.net.socketPeer(conn)}"
        	for ; true; \{
        		if hive.net.socketReceiveLine(conn) is Result.Ok(line) \{
        			_ := hive.net.socketSend(conn, "you said: \{line}\n")
        		} else \{
        			return
        		}
        	}
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	hive.net.socketServe(7000, serve)
        }
        ~CODE~

        For whole messages, prefer [WebSockets](net-websockets.md); between Hive
        programs, a [service](syslink.md) is already framed, typed and encrypted.
    `)
}

func netNames(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — names and addresses

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~resolve(name)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], NetError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~localAddress()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, NetError>~BT~ |

        A ~BT~NetError~BT~ is ~BT~NotFound~BT~, ~BT~Lookup~BT~ or ~BT~NoAddress~BT~.

        ## ~BT~resolve~BT~

        **Every** address behind a name, **in resolver order**, so a program can try
        the next when one refuses, or choose by family. It takes a **name, not an
        endpoint**: no port. **An address literal resolves to itself.**

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.net.resolve("example.com") is Result.Ok(addresses) \{
        	for each address in addresses \{
        		echo address
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## ~BT~localAddress~BT~

        The address **other machines reach this one on**: the source address the OS
        would stamp on a packet leaving by the default route. **Nothing is sent.**
        **Loopback is never an answer**: a machine with only ~BT~127.0.0.1~BT~ answers
        ~BT~NetError.NoAddress~BT~. A [node](syslink-nodes.md) prints it for another to dial.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.net.localAddress() is Result.Ok(here) \{
        	echo "listening on \{here}:9000"
        }
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func netUrls(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — URLs and query strings

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~urlEncode(text)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — safe in a path segment and a query alike |
        | ~BT~queryParamsCodec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<QueryParamsError>~BT~ — the value that says query string |

        Neither performs I/O.

        ## ~BT~urlEncode~BT~

        Percent-encodes every byte but letters, digits, ~BT~-~BT~, ~BT~.~BT~, ~BT~_~BT~ and ~BT~~~BT~; a space
        is ~BT~%20~BT~, not ~BT~+~BT~:

        ~CODE~hive
        hive.net.urlEncode("rust & go")   // rust%20%26%20go
        ~CODE~

        ## A query string is a codec

        ~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~ goes where [~BT~hive.json.codec()~BT~](json.md) goes:
        [~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) writes a type's fields and ~BT~T.decode~BT~ reads them. The
        string has **no** leading ~BT~?~BT~:

        ~CODE~hive
        type Search \{
        	term: Str -- URL as q;
        	page: Int -- Default as 1;
        	tags: Str[dyn] -- URL as tag;
        }

        encode(Search("rust & go", 2, ["web", "cli"]), hive.net.queryParamsCodec())
        // q=rust%20%26%20go&page=2&tag=web&tag=cli

        if Search.decode("q=hive+lang&tag=web", hive.net.queryParamsCodec()) is Result.Ok(search) \{
        	echo search.term   // hive lang, on page 1
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **~BT~URL as~BT~ names the parameter**, as ~BT~JSON as~BT~ names a key; otherwise the key
          is the field's name. Two fields may not answer to one key.
        * **A vector is its key repeated**, once per element, in order. An absent key is
          an empty vector; a ~BT~T[n]~BT~ needs the key exactly ~BT~n~BT~ times.
        * **A missing field is an error** unless it has a ~BT~Default~BT~; so is a key given
          twice for a field that is not a vector.
        * ~BT~+~BT~ and ~BT~%20~BT~ both read as a space, a leading ~BT~?~BT~ is tolerated, and an
          undeclared parameter is ignored. A ~BT~Bool~BT~ is ~BT~true~BT~ or ~BT~false~BT~.
        * **Only a flat type travels**: no variants, each field a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~,
          ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~, or a vector of one. Anything else is a compile error at the
          ~BT~encode~BT~ or ~BT~decode~BT~ that asked.
        * A failure is a ~BT~hive.net.QueryParamsError~BT~: the ~BT~path~BT~ that failed (such as
          ~BT~$.page~BT~), what was ~BT~expected~BT~ there, and what was ~BT~found~BT~.
    `)
}

// --- hive.sql ----------------------------------------------------------------

func sql(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.sql~BT~

        **SQLite** (the pure-Go ~BT~modernc.org/sqlite~BT~ driver, compiled into the
        executable) and **PostgreSQL** (~BT~github.com/lib/pq~BT~). The one module that links
        a dependency, fetched on the first build that needs it.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~connect(driver, connString)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SqlConnection, SqlError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~pool(driver, connString, maxOpen, maxIdle)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SqlConnection, SqlError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~close(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
        | ~BT~run(conn, fragment)~BT~ | whatever the query declared its rows to be |
        | ~BT~unsafe(conn, text)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, SqlError>~BT~ |

        * The driver is a ~BT~hive.sql.DatabaseDriver~BT~: ~BT~.SQLite()~BT~, ~BT~.PostgreSQL()~BT~ or
          ~BT~.Other(name)~BT~. A ~BT~SqlConnection~BT~ is **opaque**.
        * **Queries are [~BT~query~BT~ declarations](../queries.md)**: calling one answers
          with a ~BT~hive.sql.Fragment<rows>~BT~ (the text and its bound values, not yet run),
          and ~BT~run~BT~ runs it.
        * SQL you assemble yourself goes through ~BT~unsafe~BT~: untyped (a ~BT~Table~BT~, header
          row and all), never prepared, and outside every
          [table law](../laws.md#a-tables-laws), so nothing checks it.

        ~CODE~hive
        proc main(): void \{
        	opened := hive.sql.connect(hive.sql.DatabaseDriver.SQLite(), "./hive.db")
        	if opened is Result.Error(why) \{
        		echo "cannot open the database: \{why.message}"
        		hive.term.exit(1)
        	}
        	if opened is Result.Ok(db) \{
        		if hive.sql.run(db, findUser("ada")) is Result.Ok(rows) \{
        			for each user in rows \{
        				echo user.name
        			}
        		}
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        ## Connections and statements

        * **A ~BT~SqlConnection~BT~ is a connection pool**, safe to hold for the life of the
          program and to share across virtual threads. **Open it once in ~BT~main~BT~ and
          pass it along; never open one per query.** A handler gets it by partial
          application: ~BT~hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle(db, _))~BT~.
        * **~BT~run~BT~ prepares a statement the first time its text runs, and reuses it**,
          once per pooled connection. There is nothing to hold or close; ~BT~close(conn)~BT~
          releases them with the pool.
        * A [~BT~WHERE~BT~ block](../queries.md) writes one text per combination of its
          conditions. A connection keeps 512 statements; a text past that runs
          unprepared, as does one the database will not prepare (PostgreSQL refuses a
          script of several statements).
        * PostgreSQL behind a pooler in *transaction* mode needs one that follows
          prepared statements: PgBouncer 1.21 or newer, with ~BT~max_prepared_statements~BT~
          set.

        ## Errors

        A ~BT~SqlError~BT~ is one of five variants, each carrying a ~BT~message~BT~:

        | variant | means |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Connection~BT~ | the database could not be opened or reached, or the connection is closed |
        | ~BT~Statement~BT~ | the statement itself failed |
        | ~BT~Shape~BT~ | the columns are not the row's fields: one fills none, or a field has none |
        | ~BT~Convert~BT~ | a cell did not fit its field's type |
        | ~BT~Broken~BT~ | a row breaks one of its type's [laws](../laws.md#a-types-laws) |

        ## In-memory SQLite and containers

        * An in-memory database belongs to the **connection**, not the process, so
          under a pool each connection gets its own. Use a shared cache,
          ~BT~pool(driver, "file::memory:?cache=shared", 8, 1)~BT~, or pin the pool to one
          connection.
        * ~BT~hive container~BT~ resolves the drivers with ~BT~go mod tidy~BT~ before compiling a
          program that opens a database; both are pure Go, so the runtime stage carries
          nothing of them.
    `)
}

// --- hive.syslink ------------------------------------------------------------

func syslink(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — what a service is

        A **service** is long-lived, owns private state only it can touch, and is
        reached through an ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ (~BT~M~BT~ is the type of message it takes) by the
        same statement in this process or on another machine. Use one for **long-lived
        shared state**: a cache, a registry, a game world, a connection nobody else may
        touch. The language has no channel, no lock and no thread handle
        ([concurrency](../concurrency.md)). A window is a service too ([ui](ui.md)).

        ~BT~spawn~BT~, ~BT~at~BT~ and ~BT~kill~BT~ are **builtins**; the module holds the node calls.
        Every call: [syslink-calls](syslink-calls.md). Across machines:
        [syslink-nodes](syslink-nodes.md).

        ## The handler

        ~CODE~hive
        proc handler(state: mut State, message: Message): Message
        ~CODE~

        A handler **writes its state in place and returns the answer**, one message at a
        time. Only the service's mailbox holds the ~BT~mut~BT~ state, so no lock is needed. It
        is an ordinary ~BT~proc~BT~: a test calls it directly with a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable of its own.

        ~CODE~hive
        type Op \{
        	Add \{ word: Str }
        	Count
        	Total \{ n: Int }
        }

        proc counter(seen: mut Str[dyn], message: Op): Op \{
        	if message is Op.Add(word) \{
        		append(seen, word)
        	}
        	return Op.Total(len(seen))
        }

        proc main(): void \{
        	Str[dyn] empty = []
        	if spawn(counter, empty) is Result.Ok(cache) \{
        		async cache(Op.Add("hello"))
        		if cache(Op.Count()) is Result.Ok(reply) && reply is Op.Total(n) \{
        			echo n
        		}
        	}
        }
        ~CODE~

        * Where the service is started, the compiler checks that the first parameter is
          ~BT~mut~BT~, the starting state fits it, and the handler returns its own mailbox
          type. ~BT~spawn~BT~ answers with a ~BT~Result~BT~ (a name may be taken) holding an
          ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ read off the handler.
        * **The reply type is the mailbox type**, so nothing is annotated: one type
          declaration is the whole contract, on this machine or another.
        * **Sending a message of another type is a compile error.** Whether the service
          under a name really takes an ~BT~M~BT~ is not checked; a message that does not
          decode where it arrives fails as ~BT~Decode~BT~.
        * **Every request is answered** with what its turn returned. A caller on this
          node gets its own copy, since the next turn may write the state it was read
          from. A fire-and-forget send discards it.
        * **A ~BT~panic~BT~ in a service kills only that service**: its monitors are told, its
          callers get ~BT~Down~BT~, the node keeps running. **This is the one place ~BT~panic~BT~
          does not stop the program.** Standard error gets
          ~BT~hive: service Counter crashed: <message>~BT~.

        ## A service that knows its own address

        A handler may take a third parameter, an ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ of its own mailbox type,
        and is then handed its own address every turn, to give to work it starts so the
        result comes back as a message. ~BT~spawn~BT~ takes either shape:

        ~CODE~hive
        proc fetcher(pending: mut Int, note: Note, me: Address<Note>): Note \{
        	if note is Note.Fetch(url) \{
        		pending += 1
        		async download(me, url)
        	} else if note is Note.Fetched(_) \{
        		pending -= 1
        	}
        	return note
        }
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func syslinkCalls(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — the calls

        **Builtins**, reached with no module:

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~spawn(handler, state)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Address<M>, SyslinkError>~BT~ — starts a service |
        | ~BT~spawn(handler, state, name)~BT~ | the same, registered under ~BT~name~BT~ |
        | ~BT~at(name)~BT~ | ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ — that service on **this** node |
        | ~BT~at(endpoint, name)~BT~ | ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ — the same service on **another** |
        | ~BT~kill(address)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — stops it |

        The module's own, ~BT~hive.syslink.<call>~BT~:

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~monitor(watcher, target, notice)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
        | ~BT~listen(endpoint)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SyslinkError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~node()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
        | ~BT~peers()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ |
        | ~BT~setKey(key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

        ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ is a builtin, **opaque** type made only by ~BT~spawn~BT~ or ~BT~at~BT~;
        ~BT~Address~BT~ without its ~BT~M~BT~ is a compile error.

        A ~BT~hive.syslink.SyslinkError~BT~ is a union, each variant carrying a ~BT~message~BT~:

        | variant | means |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~Taken~BT~ | ~BT~spawn~BT~ was given a name another service holds |
        | ~BT~Down~BT~ | the service died before answering, or was already gone — at once |
        | ~BT~NoProc~BT~ | nothing is registered under that name on the node asked |
        | ~BT~Timeout~BT~ | no answer within the time given, or the default |
        | ~BT~Unreachable~BT~ | the node could not be reached |
        | ~BT~Decode~BT~ | the message did not decode as the type the service takes |
        | ~BT~NoPeer~BT~ | the address names no node to send to |
        | ~BT~NoListener~BT~ | ~BT~listen~BT~ could not listen where it was told, or this node has not listened and was asked to reach another |
        | ~BT~NoKey~BT~ | there is no cluster key to authenticate with |

        ## An address is called

        Calling it is the **only** way to reach a service; the call site decides what
        it means:

        ~CODE~hive
        async inbox(Note.Say("hi"))          // send it, wait for nothing; cannot fail
        answer := cache(Op.Count())          // send and wait: Result<Op, SyslinkError>
        answer := cache(Op.Count()) with timeout 250
        later  := async cache(Op.Count())    // wait where ~BT~later~BT~ is read
        both   := await [a(m), b(m)]         // one barrier, one deadline
        ~CODE~

        * A fire-and-forget send **cannot fail**.
        * A request to an ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ carries an ~BT~M~BT~ and answers with
          ~BT~Result<M, SyslinkError>~BT~; a message of any other type is a compile error.
        * **~BT~with timeout~BT~ folds into the same error**: still ~BT~Result<M, SyslinkError>~BT~,
          failing as ~BT~SyslinkError.Timeout~BT~, with no second ~BT~Result~BT~ around it.

        ## Names and addresses

        * A name is an **atom**, given at **spawn**, and is one service's at a time on a
          node: a second ~BT~spawn~BT~ under a name still held fails as ~BT~Taken~BT~. A ~BT~spawn~BT~
          with no name is always ~BT~Ok~BT~.
        * **~BT~at~BT~ performs no I/O and cannot fail**: it builds an address, not a lookup.
          Nothing is dialled until something is sent.
        * **~BT~M~BT~ comes from where the address lands**, as ~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~'s does: a
          declaration, or (bound with ~BT~:=~BT~) the first later send, ~BT~x := at(#Cache)~BT~ then
          ~BT~x(Op.Get("k"))~BT~. Once settled it never changes.
        * **Only a named address survives its service being restarted**; the address of
          a service with no name is that instance only.

        ~CODE~hive
        if spawn(counter, empty, #Cache) is Result.Error(syslink.SyslinkError.Taken(_)) \{
        	echo "somebody else is #Cache"
        }

        Address<Op> here  = at(#Cache)                    // this node
        Address<Op> there = at("10.0.0.4:9000", #Cache)   // another
        ~CODE~

        ## Watching and killing

        * ~BT~monitor(watcher, target, notice)~BT~ posts ~BT~notice~BT~, one of the watcher's
          messages, to ~BT~watcher~BT~ when ~BT~target~BT~ dies. The watcher is a service on this
          node, such as ~BT~at(#Supervisor)~BT~ or the address a handler is handed; the target
          may take anything. Supervisors are written this way.
        * ~BT~kill(address)~BT~ ends a service, on this node or another. Its name is free
          again before its monitors hear of it, so a supervisor can spawn the next one
          under it at once.
    `)
}

func syslinkNodes(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — across machines

        The same statement reaches a local service and a remote one; only the address
        differs: ~BT~at(#Cache)~BT~ against ~BT~at("10.0.0.4:9000", #Cache)~BT~.

        | call | answers with |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~listen(endpoint)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SyslinkError>~BT~ |
        | ~BT~node()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the endpoint this node answers on |
        | ~BT~peers()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ — the endpoints it is connected to |
        | ~BT~setKey(key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — this program's cluster key, from the next connection on |

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.syslink.listen("0.0.0.0:9000") is Result.Error(why) \{
        	echo "cannot listen: \{why.message}"
        	hive.term.exit(1)
        }
        echo "this node is \{hive.syslink.node()}"
        ~CODE~

        * **~BT~listen~BT~ answers with the endpoint a peer should dial.** Port ~BT~0~BT~ lets the
          kernel choose; the answer, and ~BT~node()~BT~ from then on, carries the chosen
          port. The host is left as written; the port is bound on every interface,
          except with a loopback host, which is bound as written.
        * **A node has no name**, only the endpoint it is dialled at, so a peer list is
          ordinary runtime data. A node prints [~BT~hive.net.localAddress()~BT~](net-names.md)
          for another to reach it.

        ## On the wire

        * **One persistent, multiplexed connection per node pair**, carrying
          length-prefixed frames, **dialled lazily**: building an address opens nothing.
        * Messages cross as **JSON**, with the codecs [~BT~hive.json~BT~](json.md) derives, so
          ~BT~JSON as~BT~ annotations decide the wire form. A mailbox type JSON cannot carry
          cannot travel: **a map does not**; use ~BT~toTable~BT~.
        * **Every frame carries a 32-bit structural digest of the message type**, so a
          peer built from a different declaration **fails loudly instead of decoding
          another type's bytes**. Both nodes must be built from the same mailbox
          declaration. A field enters the digest **under its JSON key**; a ~BT~Default~BT~
          stays out.
        * **Every connection is TLS 1.3, mutually authenticated, with no plaintext
          path**, and nothing to turn on or off.

        ## The cluster key

        Both ends of a connection prove they hold it; nodes with different keys turn
        each other away. It is the last ~BT~setKey~BT~ the program made, else
        ~BT~HIVE_SYSLINK_KEY~BT~, else ~BT~~/.hive/syslink.key~BT~, created on first use.

        ~CODE~hive
        if hive.env.getSecret("CLUSTER_KEY") is Result.Ok(key) \{
        	hive.syslink.setKey(key)
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **~BT~setKey~BT~ is held in memory only**: never written to ~BT~syslink.key~BT~, never put
          in the environment.
        * **It applies from the next connection on**, made or accepted; an open one
          stays open.
        * ~BT~setKey(bypass(""))~BT~ panics: an empty key proves nothing.

        ## Delivery is best-effort

        **Messages queued when a node is declared down are dropped**: a request gets an
        error, a fire-and-forget send gets nothing. Anything that must arrive is a
        request whose answer the caller checks; ~BT~monitor~BT~ tells a service that something
        it depends on has gone. ~BT~with timeout~BT~ bounds a call to a machine that may not
        answer:

        ~CODE~hive
        Address<Op> far = at(endpoint, #Cache)
        if far(Op.Count()) with timeout 250 is Result.Error(syslink.SyslinkError.Timeout(_)) \{
        	echo "no answer in time"
        }
        ~CODE~
    `)
}

func audio(): Str {
    return page.backticked(`
        # ~BT~hive.audio~BT~

        A recording or a **live stream** (an internet radio station, say) outside a
        scene, played by name until it is stopped.

        | call | answers |
        | --- | --- |
        | ~BT~play(name, url, volume)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
        | ~BT~stop(name)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

        ~CODE~hive
        if inCar && radioOn \{
        	hive.audio.play("radio", "https://example.com/live.mp3", 0.5)
        } else \{
        	hive.audio.stop("radio")
        }
        ~CODE~

        * **The name is the stream's identity**, as a sound's is in a
          [scene](ui-scene-sound.md). ~BT~play~BT~ under a name already playing the same ~BT~url~BT~
          only sets its ~BT~volume~BT~ (0 to 1), so calling it every frame restarts nothing; a
          different ~BT~url~BT~ replaces the stream. ~BT~stop~BT~ on a name not playing does nothing.
        * **The program's window plays it** with the platform's decoders (MP3, AAC, Ogg,
          whatever it reads), buffering the stream and picking it up again a moment
          after it drops.
        * **Nothing is heard until a window is open**; a window opened later plays
          whatever is wanted by then. A window is silent until somebody has touched it;
          a refused stream starts on the next key or tap.
        * A window the program draws itself plays through its scene's engine: MP3
          streams, and recordings in MP3, Ogg Vorbis, FLAC or WAV. One with no scene
          plays nothing.
        * ~BT~hive.audio~BT~ uses nothing of ~BT~hive.ui~BT~; a program that never calls it carries
          none of it.
    `)
}
